Round 23 (2026-02-27)

What landed
- Files changed:
  - `clang/heap/alloc.c`
  - `clang/aot/_.c`
  - `clang/wnf/alo_dup.c`
  - `clang/wnf/dup_lam.c`
  - `clang/wnf/dup_sup.c`
  - `clang/wnf/alo_lam.c`
  - `clang/wnf/uns.c`
  - `clang/wnf/dup_nod.c`
  - `clang/wnf/app_mat_ctr.c`
- Goal of this round: remove always-on allocator attribution overhead and classify the dominant compiled `UNCLASSIFIED` heap traffic.

Core implementation
1) Telemetry-off fast path at allocator boundary
- `heap_alloc_kind(size, kind)` now early-returns `heap_alloc(size)` when `HVM_AOT_HEAP_ATTR` is off.
- `heap_alloc` now gates attribution behind one unlikely branch:
  - `if (__builtin_expect(aot_heap_attr_enabled(), 0)) { ...note size + kind... }`
- Effect: when attribution is disabled, allocator no longer does TLS hint reads plus two note calls on every allocation.

2) New WNF interaction heap kinds
- Added new `AOT_HEAP_KIND_*` ids in `heap/alloc.c`:
  - `WNF_ALO_DUP`, `WNF_DUP_LAM`, `WNF_DUP_SUP`, `WNF_ALO_LAM`, `WNF_UNS`, `WNF_DUP_NOD`, `WNF_APP_MAT`
- Added string names in `aot_heap_kind_name()`.

3) Tagged raw `heap_alloc` in hot WNF interactions
- Converted these sites to `heap_alloc_kind(..., WNF_*)`:
  - `wnf_alo_dup`
  - `wnf_dup_lam`
  - `wnf_dup_sup`
  - `wnf_alo_lam`
  - `wnf_uns`
  - `wnf_dup_nod`
  - `wnf_app_mat_ctr`

Why this matters
- Round 22 showed huge compiled `UNCLASSIFIED` buckets (especially `gab_takl`).
- After tagging WNF sites, the previously unknown traffic becomes explicit and attributable.
- Data now points to the true hot family: `WNF_DUP_NOD` (not generic unknown allocator noise).

